# SOS Sang 229 — team conventions (Hackathon Cursor Bénin)

## Security (non-negotiable)

- Never commit secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, Twilio credentials, JWT secrets.
- `.env` is gitignored. Only `.env.example` (variable names + comments) may be committed.
- Do not paste credentials into source, comments, README, tickets, or seed files.
- Prefer private-safe defaults in docs (localhost URLs, empty placeholders).

## Sensitive donor / health data

- Phone numbers, GPS coordinates, blood group, hospital/patient names are sensitive.
- Never log them in cleartext (no print/console/logger of raw phone, GPS, or blood group).
- Never commit real seed data. Demo seeds must be obviously fictional
  (e.g. display name `Donneur Demo`, blood group examples only in UI placeholders).
- Do not use real Beninese phone numbers, real hospital patient names, or real home GPS.

## Git / PR

- Work on a feature branch. Naming: `<type>/<short-name>`
  Examples: `init/setup-projet`, `feat/donor-register`, `fix/healthcheck`.
- Types: `init`, `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`.
- `main` is protected by convention: no direct commits. Open a pull request.
- Keep PRs small and scoped. Describe how to run locally when the change affects setup.

## Code style (hackathon-friendly)

- Prefer simple, readable code over heavy frameworks.
- Frontend: React (JavaScript) + Tailwind. Scaffold screens first; polish later.
- Backend: FastAPI. Keep routers / models / schemas separated.
- Out of scope until explicitly tasked: full PostGIS migrations, real Twilio, full JWT,
  polished UI, production deploy configs with secrets.
